;;; A fact's nested argument splits a name that starts with `neq` — **W22**,
;;; register row `W22/kw-neq-in-fact` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`): a reserved
;;; word is a keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(box (neqw a))`, whose argument is the nested fact
;;; `(neqw a)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `neqw` is a name like any other. Root holds
;;; `(box (neqw a))` and `(p a)`. The blind enumerator proposes `(box a)`, and
;;; R is two states: the answer of `kw-neq-in-fact-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the nested `(neqw a)` as `(neq
;;; w a)`, so root held `(box (neq w a))`. `w` became a name of the program, H
;;; was `(box a)`, `(box w)` and `(p w)`, and R was their eight subsets. (`ein
;;; solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle`
;;; the same.) A keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary `grammar.md` § 1
;;; states (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation box Node :why "{?1} is in a box")
(p a)
(box (neqw a))
